  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe need to find a step function h(x) on [0,2] that satisfies the integral constraint \u222bh(x)dx=1 and minimizes C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx. The existing solution achieves C\u22480.380862, and we aim to improve this by exploring a more efficient optimization strategy that leverages the structure of the problem while ensuring feasibility. The key bottleneck in the previous approach is the use of generic gradient-based methods that may get stuck in local optima. Our new approach uses gradient information more effectively in a line-search manner but with carefully designed regularization to maintain robustness and explore a broader solution space. We will prioritize solutions that balance high quality with computational efficiency, using a hybrid strategy of local optimization that is both faster and more likely to find improved solutions.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: h as an n-point step function vector.\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx.\n- Constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1 (i.e., sum(h) * dx = 1 with dx=2/n).\n- Optimization: Use a transformed objective that helps gradient-based search escape local minima by focusing on the most restrictive lags. Define transform\u209c(h) = max(0, C\u2085(h) - c_base) to make gradient information more informative for escaping poor regions.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: n samples and optional parent solution h0 (if provided).\n2. Validate input: if parent_h is available (n=500), use it as base. Otherwise, generate random starts.\n3. Optimization phases for one n:\n   a. Line-Search Gradient Descent:\n      - Compute gradient g = grad_smooth_max(h, n) \n      - Use Newton's method on the line h + t*w (where w is a descent direction) to find best t with line_search routine\n      - Update h = h + t*w and project to feasible set\n   b. Local search: \n      - Apply constraint-preserving projections using a separable dual approach\n      - Use Lagrange multipliers to enforce integral constraint\n   \n   c. Smoothed constraint handling: \n      - Transform integral constraint to penalty function p(h) = (1/n) * \u2211(h[i] - 0.5)\u00b2\n      - Update: h = np.clip(h, 0, 1) + penalty_term/\u03bb\n   \n   d. Interpolation step:\n      - If n increases, use bilinear interpolation between consecutive points\n      - If n decreases, use nearest-neighbor averaging\n\n4. Parallel evaluation:\n   - In each iteration, evaluate C\u2085 only for the most promising candidates\n   - Use vectorized operations with NaN filters for invalid candidates\n\n5. Termination conditions:\n   - Time budget: 1100 seconds\n   - No improvement in C\u2085 after 5 evaluations\n   - Integral constraint violation detected\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Initial steps: Start with parent solution if available, else random binary with golden ratio\n2. Average n: 600\n3. Optimization focus: Start with aggressive gradient descent, then move to more cautious steps\n4. n variation: Only use n from the parent (if available) and n\u00b1100 if time allows\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Objective evaluation time dominant factor: O(n log n) for FFT operations in correlation\n- Total evaluation cost proportional to n\u00b2 (from correlation modes)\n- Fallback: Return parent if optimization fails\n- Early termination: Monitor solution quality plateau\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Check sum(h) is close to n/2\n2. Verify all h[i] between 0 and 1\n3. Re-evaluate C\u2085 with exact, same method\n4. Output format: (h_values, C\u2085, n_points)\n\nThe algorithm ensures both correctness and performance by carefully balancing exploration and exploitation, with adaptive learning from previous solutions while respecting the optimization constraints. It maintains mathematical soundness by:\n- Using properly defined gradients via vector calculus\n- Performing constraint projection in separable form\n- Applying regularization to maintain stability\n- Employing exact multidimensional search\n\nImplementation complexity is balanced to fit within the time budget and library constraints while achieving the mathematical requirements.",
